notes.body:
working with this json+ daily, and found a gap when trying to look at json like this:
{
    "web.operator.com": {
        "5gQosProfile": {
            "5qi": 96,
            "arp": {
                "preemptCap": "no",
                "preemptVuln": "yes",
                "priorityLevel": 354
            }

    },
    "web.operator.com": {
        "5gQosProfile": {
            "5qi": 97,
            "arp": {
                "preemptCap": "no",
                "preemptVuln": "yes",
                "priorityLevel": 354
            }

    },
    "voice": {
        "5gQosProfile": {
            "5qi": 99,
            "arp": {
                "preemptCap": "no",
                "preemptVuln": "yes",
                "priorityLevel": 354
            }

    }
}
The web.operator.com, voice, etc are actually a value I need to be able to filter on. Previously it was just treated as absolute, so I would have to absolutely match each and every combination. Which in a large operator, that would be impossible to perceive. It would also cause duplication of xml to have to be built under each match. Using wildcardfield I can match on .*com|voice and give that an alias of json.dnn using the aliaspath= option. Now I can filter on the key name using json.dnn="voice" and also it's one single xml block to dissect all 3 blocks. 

doing this I had discovered that xml being loaded globally kind of causes an issue. It could/would match on that regex, even though the <protocol> portion of the xml didn't match the packet. So I had to move the scope of the xml to match on the per packet instead of globally.

While I was at it, I noticed I didn't have a way to filter my <protocol>. While I added it to the protocol column display name, there was not simple way to just filter to nudm_sdm packets as an example. I didn't want to add it to the root, like there is with just typing http as the filter and getting all http packets. I appended it to json. filter. so now the xml file loads a field behind json. with the protocol name. You'll notice code to sanitize that, as to not all 3gpp as the json.3gpp, because it can't start with a digit. etc.

